  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front brings the return of heavy rain and \ngusty winds Sunday night and Monday.  \n\nThe mid-level ridge slowly breaks down over the next 24 to 48 hours \nas an anomalous low digs into the Ohio Valley and becomes cutoff. \nThis will mean slow movement east placing SNE and the whole eastern \nseaboard beneath deep, moist SW flow. As a 30-45kt LLJ develops it \nadvects 2+inch PWATs overhead (2 SD above normal). This combined \nwith impressive dynamics from the cold front, placement beneath the \nRRQ of a 120kt upper jet, and some elevated instability will lead to \na washout for Monday featuring widespread rain, heavy at times, \nalong with gusty winds as that LLJ gets mixed down. Specific \nlocations of heaviest rainfall totals are difficult to place at this \njunction, but a widespread half inch to inch of rain is likely, with \nensembles showing chances of 2+ inches essentially nil. That being \nsaid, in this super moist airmass any given location could pick up a \nquick hitting 1\"+ that may lead to street flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Still unsettled late in the week with periods of \nshowers and storms. \n\nBy Tuesday the trough axis moves into the maritimes and mid level \nridging/building surface high pressure should keep rain at bay for \nthe most part. Uncertain as to exactly when our next bout of \nunsettled weather arrives but the synoptic pattern looks to be \nactive, so periodic shortwaves (rain/t-storm chances) are expected \nto move through mid to late week, and even continuing into the \nweekend for daily afternoon/evening rain chances.",
